    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-24-the-small-real-original-web",
      "title": "The small, real, original web",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://spacetimetech.wordpress.com/2026/07/19/the-small-real-original-web/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49038977\nPoints: 8\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "The ‘small’ web, the ‘slow’ web, or the ‘indie’ web – the non corporate, original style internet we knew and loved in the 90s and early 2000s (people just writing blogs and making their own websites) before corporations and algorithms seemed to move in and take everything over (Facebook, Twitter, etc), is still out there — it still exists. Websites are not going to die, even with all the new ‘AI’ crap going around. Because so many of us still like the internet.\nThis great piece by Terry Godier about the ‘boring’ internet is a must read, it goes through everything really well, and shows a solid history of the web. A lot of us know some of the history, but admittedly, there’s so much more to it than I knew. I love how Terry goes through that history in his piece, simply, from when there were no advertising dollars at all, at the start, to today, with the glut of advertising all over the web– the thin corporate layer on top, as he says. Like a crappy crust.\nI’m not wanting a full return to the style of early websites, like the 90s version of imdb.com below. The simplicity and readability, the clean look minus ads, absolutely. But this post isn’t going to be a nostalgia trip, it’s going to be more about practical ways to keep and maintain our own sites with the internet of today– basically, a return to the feel of the more simple, human-made web of the past, minus the corporate garbage.\nThere’s a great page at Indieweb.org on what the ‘small’ web is, its history, and lots of links like ones to Ben Hoyt’s piece on it, and this piece on rediscovering the small web. Or the ‘analog’ web.\nSo, call it the small web, the slow web, the indie web, the real web, the original web, the analog web, or something else- the name doesn’t matter. The important thing is the attitude, the look, the function. Punk rock style. Folk technology. After all, the web was built on usenet, FTP, forums, and mailing lists– not profit.\nFirst, I’ll briefly mention the browsers you can use for perusing websites and making your small web experience better. Firefox is obviously fine, or its fork Waterfox. Or, if you really want something potentially better in a browser, something more private, or just different, Vivaldi, Zen, or TOR are browsers to try (skip Brave). And obviously you’ll want a VPN too (skip Mullvad) — Proton’s VPN works fine for that.\nI didn’t want to reinvent the wheel with this post, as there are already several spots online that are true labors of love– collections of invaluable resources about everything small web. So I figured I’d feature and focus on those.\nFar and beyond the best resource for small and personal web things, in my opinion, is the Resources List for the Personal Web that xandra and others have assembled at 32bit.cafe. It has basically everything. I must’ve had at least 20 or 30 more links for this post, but I skipped including them here, because they’re all listed and linked to on that resources list (control-F on the page to search it, of course). The Resources List has an excellent ‘webmastery’ section, which includes all types of resources for making your own site, including guides for RSS and more, code generators, directories (including well known ones like personalsit.es, ooh, Curlie, my friend Tom‘s bukmark.club, etc) for both inspiration and for getting your own site(s) listed there, web hosts (Neocities!), webrings, and indie alternatives to big corpo things including search (Kagi, wiby, Marginalia).\nThere are so many other great things out there for the small web, too. Teclis is a search that uses both Kagi and Marginalia results– it’s made by a founder of Kagi. A site that gives you a random wiby personal website is wiby.me/surprise. At theforest.link, it shows a random link from their listing of websites, and you can add your own. At openmentions.com, you can tag yourself in mentions from webmention or ActivityPub. There’s Raw Web search. There’s a low-tech, solar powered webzine. There’s a punk webring. There’s yet another directory of personal websites. And so on.\nI’ve been making weird things on the ‘small’ web since the 1990s. Currently, I run some of my own things, websites (deadscorpionranch.com, Mr. Beamer’s Old Timey Web Portal) and other blogs and such. These days, I use porkbun to register my TLDs. The last couple sites I’ve done, I put them on Neocities. I also started a blog on Bear. So I can personally vouch for all those services. They’re great.\nAnother consideration for the small web is of course the fediverse, and the decentralized services the fedi encompasses. I’m on Mastodon. And there’s PeerTube for video (like a decentralized YouTube) — Sepia Search and fedi.video are great places to search PeerTube, Spectra is a PeerTube instance, and you can even search Mastodon for PeerTube things via the hashtag #FeaturedPeerTube. Lemmy is like a decentralized reddit. Lemmy instances such as lemmy.world and lemmy.today are great spots to check out.\nStefan has a great website called Join the Fediverse that really gives a great overview of decentralized services. Another great piece on the fediverse is this one. The fediverse is grassroots.\nMaking your own personal blog and website (such as one of my favorites, the one my friend rootcompute runs at https://rootcompute.neocities.org) is huge — and then linking to your friends’ sites, or writing your own blog and linking back to other blogs in a blogroll— that’s a big part of the small web, too and it fosters discoverability. I have so many favorite blogs — from Hails and Ales to that html blog, ploum’s, Evan Boehs’, The Reed and Pickup, Thomas, muesli, and Jens Oliver Meiert’s. Some people call their own sites/blogs/areas digital gardens. Some call them digital garages. As long as it’s an unpolished, human-made website, it counts. Or it could be something like just lots of little HTML pages.\nBlogs are great, because you can run a very specific blog like the one I run here, for cartoons, or a more general one, such as where you’re reading this post right now. Niche blogs are everywhere, and they’re amazing. You probably have favorite blogs of your own. There are aggregators like this one or the one at Bubbles.town that help you find other blogs. You know– for discoverability! Since I’m into arcade games, one of my favorite niche blogs that’s been around a long time and that I’ve been reading for years now is the almighty Arcade Blogger.\nThere’s also a lot of blog writing prompts and challenges out there. For example, every June, Junited, something that Robert Birming started back in 2024, collects, features and shares blogs of all kinds during the month of June. Next year I should probably try and get in on that. My 2026 June was nuts.\nThe small web really is bigger than you might think. And that piece links to an excellent RSS tool, PowRSS, which is another great way to keep up on and search the indie web. You may already be familiar with it, but RSS is nothing short of amazing. RSS is one of the most important things on the web, and it’s one of the best ways to keep up with all your favorite blogs and sites– it even keeps track of podcasts. There’s one other good modern guide on everything RSS here, in addition to the one in the Resources List above. Grab a reader to start (I use gReader with Feedly). All of that’s covered in the guide(s).\nAs the first link on this post on the ‘boring’ internet by Terry Godier gets into some, even beyond the HTTP protocol, there are other small web protocols to consider. Gemini and Gopher are examples of other types of protocols- I recently wrote about the Gemini protocol here.\nI hope this has been a good, brief primer to the smaller, more real, personal, human made web and some of its best resources. This post will of course be updated, as needed, as all of my posts may.\nTo me, it’s incredibly important to remember that this real, original part of the web exists, and to use it for our own purposes- to really make the web ours (again). The real, original web was here long before the corporate shitheads came in with their agendas, and it’ll be here long after the corporate crap fades away.\nI’d love to hear your own thoughts on the small web. Leave me a comment below!",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "speckx",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-24T17:28:59.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://spacetimetech.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/internet-sign.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://spacetimetech.wordpress.com/2026/07/19/the-small-real-original-web/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 7,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-24-pogacar-on-verge-of-fifth-tour-title-after-alpe-d-huez-win",
      "title": "Pogacar on verge of fifth Tour title after Alpe d'Huez win",
      "summary": "Defending Tour de France champion Tadej Pogacar is on the verge of winning a record-equalling fifth Tour de France after taking a storming victory on stage 19 to Alpe d'Huez.",
      "body": "Defending champion Tadej Pogacar is on the verge of winning a record-equalling fifth Tour de France after taking a storming victory on stage 19 to Alpe d'Huez.The UAE Team Emirates-XRG rider pulled more than three minutes back on a breakaway group on the hors category final climb up to the famous ski resort, which was flanked by thousands of spectators from all around the world. Pogacar, 27, is aiming to become the joint-most successful rider in the Tour's history with five wins, alongside Jacques Anquetil, Eddy Merckx, Bernard Hinault and Miguel Indurain.Pogacar has a dominant lead over Belgium's Remco Evenepoel of Red Bull-Bora-Hansgrohe of seven minutes 11 seconds, despite the Belgian's improved climbing form this year.Pogacar's Mexican team-mate Isaac del Toro remains third and in the white young riders' classification leader's jersey - nine minutes 42 seconds down.The peloton will take on Alpe d'Huez once again on Saturday for the three-week race's mountainous 'queen' stage, which includes an extra 2,000 metres of climbing.\"It was very cool to win up here - it was a crazy atmosphere all climb and I'm thankful for the team that I it could pull it off,\" said Pogacar.\"When I heard some of the guys [were] dropped when [team-mate] Felix [Grosschartner] set a super good pace at the beginning of the climb, I was already going full gas, as the breakaway was so strong. Every kilometre of Alpe d'Huez I had good legs and not many could follow [me].\" Pogacar crossed the line six seconds ahead of France's Lenny Martinez of Bahrain-Victorious, who thumped his handlebars severnal times in frustration as he crossed the line.Ecuador's in-form rider Richard Carapaz of EF Education-Easypost, who won Thursday's stage to Ocieres Merlette, finished a further three seconds behind in third.A 40-plus strong breakaway had established a lead over the peloton and yellow-jersey wearer Pogacar by several minutes mid-stage, but the main pack began to slowly reel them in.It was Martinez, the USA's Sepp Kuss of Visma-Lease a Bike and a typically animated Carapaz who eventually emerged as the three seemingly set to battle for victory as they began Alpe d'Huez's legendary twisting ribbons of tarmac.But Pogacar had other ideas and set a record time on the final climb of 35 minutes 27 seconds - beating the time set by Italy's legendary Marco Pantani in 1997. Read more: Why are cycling's anti-doping tests being carried out at night?In setting his staggering time, Pogacar had taken off alone in pursuit of the remaining riders in the break, before finding rejuvenated British team-mate Adam Yates further up the climb to help pace him some of the way through the crowds, who were spilling dangerously on to the road within millimetres of the riders, who in turn were becoming ever more frustrated by the potential of a collision.Yates, 33, whose career includes a Tour stage win and time in the yellow jersey, showed great strength in leading the breakaway before becoming the perfect 'satellite' rider to help Pogacar on his late-stage charge. It comes just a few days after Yates fell ill and vomited after stage 16's time trial.He spent the subsequent two stages riding slowly at the very back of the field, losing nearly an hour in total overall.Typically riders would abandon the Tour in that kind of condition to avoid causing further illness, but Yates' tenacity did not go unnoticed.\"Adam gave me a good moral boost,\" said Pogacar. \"I was so happy he is finally back after the day of the TT - he was so bad and I'm super happy to ride behind him on the climb.\"Yates, 33, has been a loyal domestique to Pogacar since 2023, and is contracted for another two seasons. His identical twin Simon retired in January after winning the 2025 Giro d'Italia three-week Grand Tour to add to his 2018 Vuelta a Espana victory.Further down the field Denmark's Mads Pedersen of Lidl-Trek remains it the green points jersey after stretching his lead over Belgium's Jasper Philipsen.Carapaz's combative riding sees him take over the polka-dot King of the Mountains jersey by one point from Pogacar.Tour de France stage 19 results 1. Tadej Pogacar (Slo/UAE Team Emirates-XRG) 3hrs 17mins 57secs2. Lenny Martinez (Fra/Bahrain-Victorious) +6secs3. Richard Carapaz (Ecu/EF Education-Easypost) +9secs4. Sepp Kuss (USA/Visma-Lease a Bike) +1min 14secs5. Tobias Halland Johannessen (Nor/Uno X Mobility) +2min 7secs6. Remco Evenepoel (Bel/Red Bull-Bora-Hansgrohe) +2min 29secs7. Isaac del Toro (Mex/UAE Team Emirates-XRG) +2mins 41secs8. Mattias Skjelmose (Den/Lidl-Trek)  +2mins 45secs9. Paul Seixas (Fra/Decathlon-CMA CGM) same time10. Harold Tejada (Col/XDS-Astana) +3mins 22secsTour de France general classification after stage 191. Tadej Pogacar (Slo/UAE Team Emirates-XRG) 67hrs 53mins 2. Remco Evenepoel (Bel/Red Bull-Bora-Hansgrohe) +7mins 11secs3. Isaac del Toro (Mex/UAE Team Emirates-XRG) +9mins 42secs4. Paul Seixas (Fra/Decathlon-CMA CGM) +10mins 6secs5. Lenny Martinez (Fra/Bahrain-Victorious) +13mins 6. Mattias Skjelmose (Den/Lidl-Trek) +13mins 9secs7. Juan Ayuso (Spa/Lidl-Trek) +15mins 58secs8. Richard Carapaz (Ecu/EF Education-Easypost) +21mins 15secs9. Tom Pidcock (GB/Pinarello-Q36.5) +21mins 30secs10. Jordan Jegat (Fra/Total Energies) +23mins 21secs",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-24T17:17:42.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/7bc5/live/f1ad46d0-877e-11f1-a6cf-7dd9e08d50d6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/cycling/articles/c2elrwxe941o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
