      "id": "bbc-2026-07-23-indonesian-babies-were-trafficked-to-singapore-what-happens-",
      "title": "Indonesian babies were trafficked to Singapore. What happens to them now?",
      "summary": "At least 12 babies have been trafficked from Indonesia to Singapore, but what will happen to them remains unclear.",
      "body": "Indonesian babies were trafficked to Singapore. What happens to them now?Figure caption, Watch: A baby trafficking ring has been convicted - but what now for the babies?ByTessa WongReporting fromSingapore and Astudestra AjengrastriReporting fromJakartaOn Tuesday an Indonesian court convicted 19 people for baby trafficking, wrapping up a high-profile case - but leaving a key question unanswered.The members of the trafficking syndicate were found guilty of procuring at least 34 babies and selling them for adoption.Most of the children were bound for Singapore, and at least 12 babies were sent over.The case has garnered attention in both countries for months. Now that it has ended, its verdict has sharpened the question of what will happen to the babies in Singapore. Will they - and should they - return to Indonesia?This is foremost in the minds of the adoptive parents in Singapore taking care of the babies. For months they have been on tenterhooks, waiting to find out whether they will get to keep their children.Speaking to the BBC after the verdict, David, the Singaporean parent whom we interviewed previously, said he wanted both governments to make a decision soon.\"Since the case is concluded, how can we move on and what will happen to our son? It's been almost a year of fear and anxiety from waiting,\" said David, who spoke to us on condition we use a pseudonym for him.\"I sincerely hope that both authorities can come to a decision based on the best interests of the children.\"We respectfully ask that, in making this decision, consideration be given to the fact that both our son and our family are victims of the circumstances surrounding this case.\"David and his wife Ally had previously told the BBC that they had no idea the child they adopted from Indonesia could have been trafficked. They said they had done their best to do their own background checks.During the adoption process they had submitted to numerous checks from the Singaporean government which, they said, should have detected the trafficking.Neither Indonesian nor Singaporean authorities gave definitive answers to the BBC's previous queries about what they intend to do with the babies.Following the verdict, we asked them again.Indonesia's foreign affairs ministry said \"several aspects require further examination including the children's legal status, the identities of their biological parents, and the ongoing proceedings in Singapore\".It reiterated that it is \"committed to prioritising the best interests of the children\".Singaporean authorities have yet to respond to our latest queries.Image source, BBC IndonesianImage caption, Earlier this week a court in West Java found 19 people guilty of trafficking the babiesThere is little data on what happens to children who have been trafficked within Indonesia after they are rescued, as the information is generally not made public, according to Ni Made Martini Puteri, a criminologist and lecturer at the University of Indonesia.It is even less clear what happens to children who have been trafficked overseas.Anti-trafficking and child rights groups in Indonesia have argued that the children in this case should return to their biological parents, as a matter of principle.Speaking to BBC Indonesian after the verdict, Ai Maryanti, a commissioner with Indonesia's independent child protection commission, said that as the children's true identities have still not been established, their \"best interests have not been given sufficient consideration\".Just because the children may already be adopted in Singapore, that \"should not be treated as the final outcome\", she added.\"Of course, children who are victims of human trafficking must be returned. Even if they're already adopted, what's the legal basis for that?\"What we don't want is Singapore saying that the adoptions complied with their law. According to our law, we found there was a high level of manipulation, resulting in Singapore easily accepting [the adoption applications].\"During the trial it emerged that the trafficking ring had submitted false information when applying for the infants' birth certificates and family cards. They had listed the names of members of the trafficking ring as the children's birth mothers.Local officials had issued the documents based on this information, raising questions about whether they failed to conduct adequate checks.Uncharted territoryAnnulling the adoptions in Singapore and returning the children to Indonesia will likely be complicated, as it goes into uncharted territory.Lawyers told the BBC they had never heard of a case where an adoption order was revoked in Singapore.With a framework that is designed with \"substantial safeguards upfront\" including numerous checks, \"adoption orders in Singapore are generally treated as final\", said Derek Choo, an associate director with PKWA Law Practice.If the government applies to revoke the adoption orders, the court could consider several factors, said Lim Fung Peen, a consultant with Yuen Law.One is the psychological impact on the child, he said. Child psychologists had previously told the BBC that separating young children from their caregivers could have a lifelong impact on their mental health.The duration that the child has spent in Singapore, and the family and living conditions the child would return to in Indonesia, could also be considered, according to Lim. The babies are believed to have entered Singapore between 2023 and 2025, which makes the oldest among them at least three years old by now.Another factor could be whether the basis of adoption is untrue, said Lim. In this case, the babies' birth certificates have been proven to be fraudulent and thus a court could find the basis of their adoption \"questionable\", he said.Then, there are other challenges.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, At least 12 babies are believed to be in Singapore already (file picture)Indonesian authorities still have not identified all of the children's birth parents. This means that some children may have no one to return to.If they are returned, they would be placed into state care. At least eight other babies rescued from the trafficking ring are already in the care of an orphanage in Bandung.\"Suppose a child is recognised as a trafficking victim and returned to Indonesia, but nobody here knows who the biological parents are because our evidentiary system is weak,\" said Puteri.\"I would argue that if the child has only known life in Singapore since infancy, then it makes more sense for the child to remain in Singapore with the adoptive parents.\"However, if the biological parents are later identified, the child should still have the right to know who they are.\"With the verdict, Singapore should now treat these children as victims of trafficking and thus give them protection, she argued.\"Protection should also extend to the adoptive parents because they too may have had no idea what had happened,\" she added.David, the Singaporean parent, said that he and his wife only wish to \"live a normal family life, like any other couple hoping to raise a child in a safe, stable, and loving home.\"We hope that this matter can now be brought to a resolution so that our son can move forward with certainty and security.\"Additional reporting by Farida Susanty of BBC Indonesian.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
