    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-22-never-enough",
      "title": "Never Enough",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://dark.ronacher.eu/2026/7/21/never-enough/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49001472\nPoints: 81\n# Comments: 25",
      "body": "written on July 21, 2026\nabout Silicon Valley are hard to read without wondering if everyone involved has\nlost their mind.  Once a place that celebrated “the crazy ones, the misfits and\nrebels who saw things differently,” it now seems to have found a new kind of\ncrazy that isn’t born of rebellion but fear of falling behind.  Everyone is\nchasing the person ahead of them, with little time to remember where they meant\nOne story talks about a couple that earns a combined $550,000 a year and still\nfeels behind because someone at Anthropic might buy the dream house first.  The\nhusband asked his wife to take over almost all parenting so he could spend days,\nnights and weekends becoming his company’s top user of AI.  And she did.\nThe other story covers a founder who has Claude review her first dates and tell\nher whether she was engaging and empathetic enough, thanks to recording every\nsingle one of them.  Two people meet and afterwards, rather than talk to the\nother person or trust her own judgment, one of them sits alone with a machine\nand asks how well she performed.\nTechnology was supposed to make room for life but instead for more and more\npeople life is slowly being rearranged around AI.  People fear being replaced by\nmachines and respond by giving those machines more of their judgment, attention\nand time.  And for what?  Every “saved” hour is returned to a race with no\nIf the valley shows us what it means to live in the future, perhaps falling\nbehind is not the worst thing that could happen.  Maybe we need the misfits who\nknow when to stop.  Who leave a conversation unrecorded.  Who play with their\nchildren without agents running in the background.  Who can admire another\nperson’s success without feeling diminished by it.  Who find peace not by\ngetting ahead, but by no longer needing to.\nThe Great Buddha of Kamakura. (Wikimedia Commons.)",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "reasonableklout",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-22T03:24:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://dark.ronacher.eu/social/2026-07-21-never-enough-social.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://dark.ronacher.eu/2026/7/21/never-enough/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
