      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cy0j9yyed1lo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-21-consortium-in-talks-to-buy-liverpool-minority-stake",
      "title": "Consortium in talks to buy Liverpool minority stake",
      "summary": "A consortium led by British-Indian millionaire businessman Amit Bhatia expresses an interest in buying a minority stake in Liverpool.",
      "body": "A consortium led by British-Indian millionaire businessman Amit Bhatia has expressed an interest in buying a minority stake in Liverpool.Bhatia, the son-in-law of the Indian billionaire businessman Lakshmi Mittal, was director and co-owner at QPR for 18 seasons but stepped down from the board and relinquished ownership of the club on Tuesday afternoon.In a statement, Liverpool owners Fenway Sports Group (FSG) told BBC Sport: \"An investment consortium led, managed, and represented by Amit Bhatia has expressed interest in making a strategic minority investment in Liverpool Football Club.\"BBC Sport understands that the deal has not yet been finalised with FSG.If the investment was to proceed, it would follow a similar structure to a deal made in 2023, when FSG sold a minority stake in the Reds to global sports investment firm Dynasty.FSG purchased Liverpool in 2010 for £300m, when the club was on the verge of administration.According to the Financial Times, a deal with the consortium led by Bhatia would value Liverpool at more than $6bn (£4.5bn).BBC Sport has contacted Bhatia's representatives for comment.Amit Bhatia is a former investment banker who worked for Morgan Stanley on Wall Street before becoming an entrepreneur.He operates businesses in construction, real estate, and private equity.The construction company he founded at 32 is now the largest independent building materials business in the UK with more than 5,000 employees, and his real estate firm builds homes, student housing and offices across the country.Londoner Bhatia, 46, was awarded the young entrepreneur of the year award in 2013, and sits on the advisory board of the Saudi Arabian government's cultural affairs and international relations unit.He was a director and co-owner of QPR before his unexpected departure on Tuesday, and a stand at Loftus Road bears his surname.He married Vanisha Mittal Bhatia in 2004, in a six-day ceremony in France costing more than $55m (£41m), which at the time was recognised by Guinness World Records as the most expensive of all-time.Vanisha is the daughter of Indian steel magnate Lakshmi Mittal, whose net worth has been estimated at more $30bn (£22bn).FSG originally made clear in 2022 that it would be open to new investment in Liverpool, either from minority shareholders or a potential full sale.It said at the time: \"FSG has frequently received expressions of interest from third parties seeking to become shareholders in Liverpool.\"FSG has said before that under the right terms and conditions, we would consider new shareholders if it was in the best interests of Liverpool as a club.\"The full sale never materialised but the agreement with Dynasty in 2023 was valued at between £82m and £164m, and was hailed by FSG as helping offset bank debt accrued from infrastructure projects including the redevelopments of the Main Stand, Anfield Road end, and the club's Kirkby training ground.At the time of that deal, FSG president Mike Gordon said: \"Our long-term commitment to Liverpool remains as strong as ever.\"[We want] to further strengthen the club's financial position and sustain our ambitions for continued success on and off the pitch.\"Since then, FSG has explored the possibility of purchasing a second club in continental Europe to add to its portfolio.The multi-club model would have followed the examples of Chelsea and Manchester City, whose owners also operate clubs in other territories. But after examining potential purchases including Spanish sides Malaga and Getafe and French club Bordeaux, FSG did not proceed with any of the deals and is now understood to have moved away from the model.That decision to eschew the approach led to the departure of Michael Edwards last month - the former Liverpool sporting director had been rehired by FSG in order to lead the multi-club project.Current sporting director Richard Hughes is managing the club's transfer strategy this summer and has a contract lasting until summer 2027, but is believed to be open to a move to Saudi Arabia in the near future.Liverpool principal owner John W Henry has taken a back seat on matters relating to the club in public since acknowledging and apologising for his role in the widely derided European Super League project in 2021.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T16:37:11.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/b9f4/live/375cfcb0-851e-11f1-97e8-4f50dea2b016.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c1j1081dyn1o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-21-instagram-now-lets-you-swap-out-the-music-in-your-old-posts",
      "title": "Instagram now lets you swap out the music in your old posts",
      "summary": "With the new \"Replace Audio\" tool, users can update the music on their post at any time while keeping the post's existing likes, comments, shares, and reach intact.",
      "body": "9:15 AM PDT · July 21, 2026\nInstagram announced on Tuesday that users can now swap out the music on their published feed posts and carousels. Until now, users could only change the music on a post by deleting it and then re-uploading it.\nWith a newly launched “Replace Audio” tool, users can update the music on their post at any time while keeping the post’s existing likes, comments, shares, and reach intact. The Meta-owned company says the new feature is another step in its efforts to build tools that give creators more creative control over their content.\nThe update removes a limitation that forced creators and brands to choose between keeping a post’s engagement and updating its soundtrack. In some cases, changing a song can help keep content aligned with trends. Other times, people may want to refresh old posts to reflect their evolving music tastes.\nThe new feature is accessed by navigating to the post where you want to change the audio, then tapping the two-line menu in the top-right corner of the post. You will then need to scroll down to “Edit,” after which you’ll see the option to swap out the audio.\nInstagram has come under fire over the years for introducing controversial features that many users never wanted, so it makes sense for the social media giant to now add a small but meaningful feature that people may actually find useful.\nFor example, the social media giant recently killed a feature that allowed users to modify photos from public Instagram accounts using AI. The company said last week that the feature “missed the mark” and would be removed.\nIn May, the company released a feature called “Instants” that Instagram billed as a new format to share real-life moments as they were happening. However, many users weren’t aware of how the feature worked and ended up accidentally sending images to everyone in their Friends list, leading to many sharing their frustrations with the feature online.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nAisha is a consumer news reporter at TechCrunch. Prior to joining the publication in 2021, she was a telecom reporter at MobileSyrup. Aisha holds an honours bachelor’s degree from University of Toronto and a master’s degree in journalism from Western University.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Aisha by emailing aisha@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at aisha_malik.01 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Aisha Malik",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T16:15:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/instagram-GettyImages-2150186181.jpg?resize=1200,889",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/21/instagram-now-lets-you-swap-out-the-music-in-your-old-posts/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-21-instagram-will-let-users-endlessly-swap-the-audio-on-old-pos",
      "title": "Instagram will let users endlessly swap the audio on old posts",
      "summary": "There's a symbiotic - and sometimes frustrating - relationship between social media sites and the creators that depend on them. Platforms need influencers' and creators' content to keep consumers on their apps; the creators, in turn, need to be able to reach those audiences to justify creating their content in the first place. Creators want a seamless way to make and disseminate their content whil",
      "body": "There’s a symbiotic — and sometimes frustrating — relationship between social media sites and the creators that depend on them. Platforms need influencers’ and creators’ content to keep consumers on their apps; the creators, in turn, need to be able to reach those audiences to justify creating their content in the first place. Creators want a seamless way to make and disseminate their content while being able to make money; audiences want a bottomless feed of entertaining things to look at. And platforms need to keep both parties relatively happy.&nbsp;\nOver the last few months, Instagram has added a slew of features that are helpful for creators, but that might seem slightly bewildering to everyone else. In June, the company announced it would allow users to reorder the posts on their profile grid, shuffling up chronologically arranged static posts. Instagram followed that up with the ability to add individual captions to each slide in a photo set. And today, the company said it will allow users to replace the audio track in previously posted photos and videos with different music, without having to reupload the post with a new song. It’s a feature that regular Instagram users likely won’t find much use in.\n“Creators put a lot of thought and care into their posts, but that doesn’t mean they should be locked into every decision they made at the moment they hit publish,” Jimmy O’Keefe, head of creator product marketing at Instagram, said in an emailed statement. “We’ve been building toward this idea that your content should be able to grow and change with you — whether that’s reordering your grid, updating a caption, or now swapping out the music on a post. Replace Audio is the latest step in that direction.”\nWhen asked whether updating the audio would give content creators an algorithmic boost, O’Keefe told The Verge in an email that it wouldn’t, but that creators will be able to swap the audio track an unlimited number of times without losing the likes, comments, and shares that they’ve so far amassed. In effect, it gives them a way to retroactively tweak existing content to the viral sounds of any given moment. It also means that audiences may start seeing videos they could have sworn had a different soundtrack previously.\nThere are two ways of understanding this series of new features. One is that Instagram is making a new pitch to creators, hoping these small but potentially powerful tweaks will entice them to post on Instagram over its competitor, TikTok. The knock-on effect is that it feels more and more like the platform is tipping in the direction of creators rather than casual users.\nFor the average Instagram user, the idea of replacing last year’s song of the summer with this year’s hit is perplexing; why would you update a post from a year ago to retroactively make it fit current trends? But the feature is intriguing if you’re a content creator or an advertiser: It opens up the possibility that one piece of content can be infinitely recycled. A post that a creator was paid to add a certain song to can be updated annually with a new song, as long as the visual content itself isn’t too dated; a brand can reheat its viral post from two years ago with a trendier audio track rather than reshoot or reupload a new clip. A dancer could A/B test the same clip, but set it to a different song each week. Content becomes less a snapshot in time than an ever-evolving — and ever-monetizable — vehicle for virality.&nbsp;\nMeanwhile, the features that come with the new $3.99-per-month premium tier of Instagram seem to target users who are trying to be on Instagram the old-fashioned way: Perks include a “Story Spotlight,” which “give[s] your story priority for your friends,” the ability to send flashier heart emoji to friends, and stories that expire in 48 hours “so the people you care about have more time to see it.” (It also offers features that feed on our more base impulses, like the ability to see how many people watched your story multiple times.) In other words, for a small monthly fee, you get a better shot at reaching the people you actually know and care about.\n“Who is Instagram for?” is a question that has been swirling for years, especially as TikTok ushered in the era of short-form video. There was a time not that long ago when users tried to push back on the coming wave of Reels, as well as recommended content from accounts a user didn’t follow that replaced content from friends in their feeds — two features that were mainstays of TikTok. Now these are hallmarks of Instagram too. As the platform has changed, average people and creators alike have complained about not being able to reach their followers, the people with whom they have relationships. The concept of posting as a record of your personal life for friends, family, and acquaintances feels more and more foreign. For the growing class of “creators,” though, posting on Instagram has perhaps never made more sense.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Analysis",
        "Creators",
        "Instagram",
        "Meta",
