    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-21-samsung-can-8217-t-afford-to-play-it-safe-with-apple-8217-s-",
      "title": "Samsung can&#8217;t afford to play it safe with Apple&#8217;s first foldable looming",
      "summary": "Samsung’s latest foldable is expected to be shorter and wider than last year’s Z Fold 7. | Photo: Allison Johnson / The Verge\t\n\nTomorrow's foldable-centric Galaxy Unpacked event looks like it will be Samsung's biggest in years. The company is expected to launch new versions of its Fold and Flip phones while also introducing a brand new Fold form factor that's shorter and wider.\nIt would be the sec",
      "body": "Tomorrow’s foldable-centric Galaxy Unpacked event looks like it will be Samsung’s biggest in years. The company is expected to launch new versions of its Fold and Flip phones&nbsp;while also introducing a brand new Fold form factor that’s shorter and wider.\nIt would be the second upheaval to Samsung’s foldable lineup in less than 12 months, after last year’s Galaxy Z TriFold. For years, there was the little Flip and the big Fold, and that was enough. Not any more. With the first foldable iPhone looming on the horizon, Samsung can’t get away with playing it safe.\nLeaks have pointed to a wider Samsung foldable for months, almost as long as we’ve been told that Apple’s first foldable will be similarly squat. Samsung itself has all but admitted the new design is on the way, teasing “a new shape” for foldable phones, while its Unpacked invites show the top part of a ticket stub torn off to leave a shorter part behind.\nA wider foldable isn’t entirely new. Huawei was first to the punch this year with its passport-shaped Pura X Max, but this design goes back even further. Google’s initial Pixel Fold was wider than most, as was Oppo’s Find N, and Microsoft’s unusual dual-screen Surface Duo was even wider. Perhaps ironically, the main reason these wider foldables died out is because Samsung itself rejected the design, quickly optimizing its Fold phones toward slim outer screens and square inner ones.\n[Image: The first Pixel Fold was wide, but the design never caught on. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/24752673/DSCF2065.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nSamsung had the influence to shift the market because until now it’s been the major player in foldables. It combined a first-mover advantage with its sheer size and clout to dominate sales and largely dictate the direction of the market. It’s only in recent years that competitors’ hardware innovations, including nearly creaseless displays and reliable dustproofing, have pushed Samsung into playing catchup, but last year the company reasserted itself with the impressively thin design of the Z Fold 7 and the edge-to-edge cover screen of the Z Flip 7, its two most complete foldables yet.\nThe dynamic is about to change. There’s always a bigger fish, and in this case that’s Apple. Industry experts have long predicted that Apple’s entry into the market was the only thing that could bring foldables from a niche interest to a mainstream hardware category, and all evidence suggests that in September we’ll find out if that’s true. “The launch of Apple’s first foldable iPhone will mark a turning point for the foldable segment,” says IDC analyst Francisco Jeronimo in a report predicting the company will claim an enormous 34 percent of the foldable market by value in its first year.\nTo carry on with the aquatic metaphors, Samsung will hope that a rising tide lifts all boats, increasing the size of the foldable market for everyone. But there’s just as big a risk that Apple’s arrival leaves Samsung floundering, eating into its sales and disrupting the market dominance it’s spent seven years building.\nGetting ahead of Apple with a similar form factor seems like smart insurance. The worst case for Samsung might be unanimous agreement that the foldable iPhone is excellent, with nothing in its stable that compares. Introducing a wider foldable now eliminates at least that risk, while keeping its regular Flip and Fold designs around would provide cover in case those remain more popular.\n[Image: Samsung’s last foldable experiment, the triple-layered TriFold, was short-lived. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/01/DSC02041_processed.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nStill, aping Apple carries risks, as Samsung knows all too well. Just look at how the Galaxy S25 Edge worked out. Samsung’s slim phone arrived just months before Apple introduced the iPhone Air and must have felt like a fairly safe bet. Sales never materialized though, and even the Air has been a disappointment for Apple. Then there’s Samsung’s previous foldable experiment, the Galaxy Z TriFold, which lasted less than three months in the US market. It remains to be seen if we’ll ever see a sequel.\nWe won’t know until tomorrow exactly what Samsung has in store, and how novel its wider foldable will prove to be. It could be another two months before we see Apple’s response, and months beyond that before we know if it transforms the foldable phone market the way analysts expect. Either way, Samsung can’t afford to wait to find out. Expanding its lineup is a gamble, but the risks of sitting still could be even greater.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Apple",
        "Foldable Phones",
        "Gadgets",
        "iPhone",
        "Mobile",
        "Phones",
        "Report",
        "Samsung",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Dominic Preston",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T15:20:33.000Z",
