    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-21-is-the-internet-broken",
      "title": "Is the internet broken?",
      "summary": "75% of the world is online, and in many ways it makes all our lives better. But faced with AI slop, misinformation and toxicity, many people are asking: Is there a better way?",
      "body": "Around 75% of the world's population is online and - in many ways - this makes all our lives better. But faced with a barrage of ads, misinformation, AI slop, toxicity and doom-scrolling, it can feel like the internet is kind of... broken. What happened? And where are we headed next? Featuring interviews with: Wikipedia founder Jimmy Wales, Hatelab director Matthew Williams, author and activist Cory Doctorow and author and co-founder of Logging Off Club Adele Zeynep Walton. Big thanks to students at the University of Cardiff. Made by BBC Ideas in partnership with UKRI, externalUsing AI to tackle online hate, externalWatch the The Tech Billionaire Takeover on BBC iPlayerFind out more about Logging Off Club, externalFilm by Daniel Nils Roberts💡Watch more videos at BBC Ideas",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T15:10:43.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/1024/cpsprodpb/65c9/live/a398c870-8521-11f1-b976-0b9c15b0ccfc.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/videos/c0qv07pzyepo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
