    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-21-keeper-martinez-may-step-aside-from-argentina",
      "title": "Keeper Martinez may 'step aside' from Argentina",
      "summary": "Argentina goalkeeper Emi Martinez says he is considering whether it is \"time to step aside\" from international football after his side's 1-0 defeat to Spain in the World Cup final.",
      "body": "Argentina goalkeeper Emi Martinez says he is considering whether it is \"time to step aside\" from international football after his side's 1-0 defeat to Spain in the World Cup final.The 33-year-old has won 67 caps for his country and played every minute of Argentina's eight World Cup games, keeping two clean sheets and conceding eight goals.He was a World Cup winner in 2022, also claiming the golden glove award as the tournament's outstanding goalkeeper.\"I dreamt we'd win it again, I dreamt of bringing it back to Argentina and making history once more,\" Martinez wrote in a post on Instagram., external\"The truth is, the pain is hard to explain. There are many things to reflect on and how to move forward, and if it's time to step aside.\"I'm so sorry, I truly tried my best to help my country and my team-mates.\"Martinez won the Yashin Trophy, awarded to the world's best goalkeeper at the Ballon d'Or awards, in 2023 and 2024.He has played for Aston Villa since 2020 and has a contract until 2029, but has been linked to a move to Italian side Juventus this summer.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T13:01:20.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/9f9d/live/f3bd3c10-8502-11f1-8b9b-a724b687bb3b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c62xzle86n4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-21-the-wireless-headset-that-gets-hot-swappable-batteries-right",
      "title": "The wireless headset that gets hot-swappable batteries right",
      "summary": "New gaming gear is always promising to shave milliseconds off latency or response time. The improvements usually aren't obvious. But the Glorious GHS InfinitePlay wireless headset, on the other hand, could save you far more than just milliseconds: It has the fastest hot-swap battery system I've ever used, and the headset doesn't die when you swap out its battery either. \nGlorious improves on the f",
      "body": "New gaming gear is always promising to shave milliseconds off latency or response time. The improvements usually aren’t obvious. But the Glorious GHS InfinitePlay wireless headset, on the other hand, could save you far more than just milliseconds: It has the fastest hot-swap battery system I’ve ever used, and the headset doesn’t die when you swap out its battery either.&nbsp;\nGlorious improves on the formula popularized by SteelSeries, which made swappable, rechargeable batteries a feature worth caring about in a high-end headset. Its new $229.99 headset has zero audio downtime while swapping batteries because it has a fallback battery that can run up to three hours. This is a big upgrade over competing headsets, like the SteelSeries Arctis Nova Pro Omni and the Turtle Beach Stealth Pro 2, which disconnect the moment their batteries are removed for swapping.\nThe InfinitePlay’s battery-swapping system is faster and more foolproof, too. Its batteries insert into the right earpiece’s open slot like a cartridge, and you can’t install them incorrectly, so long as their grippy edge faces down. It’s easy to slot it in without breaking eye contact from my screen, and the audio never stops. Removing and inserting the batteries is fun, too — though battery life is long enough that I rarely had to swap them during my monthlong testing period.&nbsp;\nAlmost every aspect of using the InfinitePlay is satisfying. The notched metal knobs facing toward the back of each ear cup make it simple to adjust volume and game/chat mix, or pick up phone calls and pause music when connected to my phone via Bluetooth. I’ve been using the headset mostly with a PC, where the battery-charging base station always has a full battery ready for use. But I can yank out its USB-C 2.4GHz transmitter when I want to play on my PlayStation 5 or the Steam Deck.\nSound quality is better than I expected from a company that’s fairly new to the audio space. Its bass, clarity, and soundstage yield a pleasant listening experience. You can get better audio quality at this price point if that’s your priority (the Fractal Scape is a good alternative). But Glorious nails a lot of the basics. The headset’s near-180-degree rotating ear cups don’t pinch my head, even with glasses on, and its bungee-style headband and adjustable side arms should let most people find a comfortable fit.\nThe InfinitePlay doesn’t have everything going for it. There’s no active noise cancellation, nor does it ship with a nice hard case, and its built-in mic sounds pretty lousy (use its detachable boom mic when at all possible). There’s a companion app that’s serviceable, but I wish there was a browser-based app to make it simpler to adjust settings. There are pricier headsets that do a better job at ticking more boxes. But if you’re looking for something that’s good and just won’t die, this $230 headset embarrasses pricier options with its no-look, no-downtime swapping process.\nPhotography by Cameron Faulkner / The Verge",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gaming",
        "Hands-on",
        "PC Gaming",
        "Reviews",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Cameron Faulkner",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T13:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/268648_Glorious_GHS_InfinitePlay_headset_CFaulkner_0013.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/967153/glorious-infiniteplay-wireless-gaming-headset-hands-on",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-21-nintendo-keeps-making-shooters-more-friendly-and-approachabl",
      "title": "Nintendo keeps making shooters more friendly and approachable",
      "summary": "Nintendo does a lot of weird things, but Splatoon might just be one of the strangest - or at least the boldest. The series started as an attempt to muscle into the ultra-competitive online shooter space, but in a way that feels a world away from the violence and bombast of Call of Duty or Battlefield. Splatoon is essentially candy-coated paintball starring a race of skate punk squid creatures, and",
      "body": "Nintendo does a lot of weird things, but Splatoon might just be one of the strangest — or at least the boldest. The series started as an attempt to muscle into the ultra-competitive online shooter space, but in a way that feels a world away from the violence and bombast of Call of Duty or Battlefield. Splatoon is essentially candy-coated paintball starring a race of skate punk squid creatures, and across three entries Nintendo has just about perfected the formula. The series introduced the idea of competitive shooters to whole new audiences, and now it’s expanding: Splatoon Raiders takes it in a different direction, but with the same focus on making shooters more approachable.\nAs opposed to past entries, which primarily revolved around team-based competitive matches, Raiders is what’s known as a looter shooter. Think games like Destiny or Borderlands where you take on missions and explore dungeons in order to earn new gear and skills, which in turn let you take on more challenging missions and dungeons. It’s a genre where repetition is inherent in the premise as you replay levels over and over again in search of the best loot. This means that the moment-to-moment experience — how fun the weapons are to use, how interesting the levels are to explore, how much incentive there is to get new gear to play around with — has to be enjoyable enough to encourage those replays. And that’s exactly where Splatoon excels.\nIn terms of how it plays, Raiders is nearly identical to its predecessors. You still control a squid-like kid that can wield various weapons, ranging from guns that look like Super Soakers to gigantic paint rollers. Splatoon’s defining characteristic is ink: It’s what you shoot at enemies, but you can also transform from a humanoid into a squid in order to swim through it. Immersing yourself in ink lets you move more quickly through a level, replenish your health, and refill your ammo supply. The result is shooter action where you’re constantly moving, because standing still means death (or, in the parlance of Splatoon, getting splatted).\nThat all remains true in Raiders. But here you’re using those skills while traveling to various levels in order to defeat enemies and find treasure. The story has you playing the role of a mechanic aboard a craft that crash-lands on a mysterious chain of islands. Unable to leave, you and your fellow crew — which consists of members of the in-universe Splatoon pop group Deep Cut — set about building a rickety base and then exploring the islands in search of treasure and, hopefully, a way home.\nLike most looter shooters, the gameplay cycle works like this: You gear up at base, head into a level, return home with some improved loot, and then do it all over again. There are a few types of levels in Raiders, which get progressively more challenging, and they mostly involve either defeating a huge horde of enemies before a clock winds down or collecting a large amount of fish eggs that drop from downed foes. Some levels are brief, lasting just a few minutes, while others require more exploration. There are also boss fights against some gigantic creatures that remind me a bit of the elaborate battles in Monster Hunter games.\nEssentially, Raiders takes Splatoon’s core gameplay and transposes it into a new genre. And it remains a blast, with levels that have an incredible sense of tension; there were many times when it came down to the last few thrilling seconds, which determined whether or not I’d beat a level. And while you can play through the whole thing by yourself, Raiders also supports co-op play for up to four people.\nWhat really makes Raiders work, though, is that it also constantly encourages you to experiment. One way it does that is through the sheer amount of customization options. There is a huge range of different weapons to use — my favorite is a katana made of sponge that can splash ink — and each one can be leveled up and tweaked by adding parts that can alter its abilities, making it faster or stronger. You also have multiple powers that unlock over time, each of which provides a different benefit, like a spinning top that explodes on impact or a turret that provides cover in a heated shootout. Finding what works for you means trying out a lot of ideas. But the game also goes a step further. In order to fully level up your abilities, you actually have to try out different items and use them for a prolonged period, and some stages even have different treasure depending on what you have equipped. This keeps you from finding one kind of playstyle and sticking with it through the whole game.\nThe remarkable thing about the original Splatoon was how it took one of gaming’s most intimidating genres — competitive online multiplayer — and turned it into something incredibly approachable. Raiders does the same thing for looter shooters. It’s not a never-ending grind where you feel forced to play levels repeatedly in order to see any progress, nor is it a live-service experience intent on sucking up all of your free time and money. It takes the basic elements that have made the genre so popular — exciting gunplay, challenging levels, fine-tuned customization — and puts them in a package that respects the player and their time. At a moment when the live-service space is imploding, and new shooters fail with alarming regularity, Raiders is a reminder of why these games are so popular in the first place — as well as a sign that Splatoon still has plenty of room to grow.\nSplatoon Raiders launches on the Nintendo Switch 2 on July 23rd.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
