    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-21-tropical-storm-bertha-threatens-us-gulf-coast",
      "title": "Tropical Storm Bertha threatens US Gulf coast",
      "summary": "Storm surges up to 4ft are possible along parts of the Louisiana and Mississippi coasts, officials say.",
      "body": "A tropical storm swirling in the US Gulf is expected to bring heavy rainfall and a \"life-threatening storm surge\" to the south-east of the country, according to the National Hurricane Center (NHC).Tropical Storm Bertha was located about 105 miles (165km) south of Panama City, Florida, carrying windspeeds of 40mph (65 km/h) on Monday.\"Bertha will move near or along the northern Gulf coast during the next several days,\" said the Miami-based NHC.The storm could trigger flooding and storm surges in Alabama, Florida, Louisiana and Mississippi as it drifts west towards Texas. Some areas could receive 8in (20cm) of rain by Friday, and \"isolated\" tornados could spawn in Florida, say forecasters.In an updated bulletin on Monday evening, forecasters said that the tropical storm may build in strength over the next several days, before potentially making landfall in New Orleans, Louisiana, by Wednesday.The bulletin added that tropical storm-force winds extended 70 miles outward from the centre of the storm, and that the storm was moving north-west at around 3mph.\"A tropical storm watch is in effect for Ochlockonee River, Florida, westward to the Jefferson/Plaquemines Parish border in Louisiana,\" forecasters said.Surges are expected to be between 1-4ft along parts of the Louisiana and Mississippi coastlines, officials said. Flash flooding could occur in urban areas. The storm's arrival comes amid sweltering summer heat in the US south.In New Orleans, sandbags are being distributed to stave off flood waters. Residents have also been asked to ensure that local storm drains are clear. Bertha is forecast to weaken back to a tropical depression on Thursday and may dissipate by the end of the week as it nears the Texas coast.This is only the second named storm of the season, after Tropical Storm Arthur brought some flooding to the US last month.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T02:42:44.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ce22/live/7cb71d20-84ad-11f1-b976-0b9c15b0ccfc.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqjxzzdrl4lo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-21-anti-ice-army-veteran-set-fire-outside-manhattan-federal-bui",
      "title": "'Anti-ICE' Army veteran set fire outside Manhattan federal building, say officials",
      "summary": "Andrew Arrabaca, 43, was armed with two axes, a machete, three knives and fireworks, says the FBI.",
      "body": "A former US Army soldier has been arrested after igniting a fire in front of a federal building in New York City, says the FBI. Andrew Arrabaca, 43, allegedly shot a pellet gun and set off fireworks on Monday morning at 26 Federal Plaza, where the FBI, Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE), and US Citizenship and Immigration Services have offices.Federal agents tackled the suspect, who they say was armed with a machete and other weapons.Three people sustained minor injuries in the incident, including an immigrant who was attending a status hearing, said the FBI. The lower Manhattan site has become a flashpoint for protests against President Donald Trump's immigration policies.The suspect, whom the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) called an \"anti-ICE rioter\", launched fireworks before pouring an accelerant on the stairs of an entrance to the building, causing a fire. The BBC has contacted DHS for comment.The fire department put out the blaze.Arrabaca \"made several anti-government and anti-ICE statements during the incident\", the FBI said.FBI New York Assistant Director James Barnacle said during a news conference that Arrabaca was armed with two axes, a machete, three knives and fireworks.He fired about five to seven pellets from an airsoft gun in the direction of the building, Barnacle said.Of those injured, two were government employees, Barnacle said.DHS said a bystander was grazed, and a security officer who tackled and detained Arrabaca sustained minor injuries.\"He is an anti-American, anti-government extremist,\" Barnacle added.Arrabaca, of Poughkeepsie, New York state, served in the regular Army from 2001-04 and in the Army National Guard-New York from 2004-05, according to the BBC's US partner CBS. He did not have any deployments and left the Army with the rank of specialist.New York City Mayor Zohran Mamdani called the incident \"deeply disturbing\" in a post on X.\"Our administration will continue to ensure that every New Yorker is safe in their city - and hold accountable any who threaten that,\" he said.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-21T02:16:05.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/037f/live/212d5ff0-84a8-11f1-b976-0b9c15b0ccfc.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c1m1rr90dzxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
