      "id": "bbc-2026-07-20-uefa-president-boycotts-world-cup-final-in-protest-against-f",
      "title": "Uefa president boycotts World Cup final in protest against Fifa",
      "summary": "Uefa president Aleksander Ceferin did not attend the World Cup final between Argentina and Spain in protest against Fifa.",
      "body": "Uefa president Aleksander Ceferin did not attend the World Cup final between Argentina and Spain in protest against Fifa. While the Slovenian did travel to Mexico City for the opening game between the co-hosts and South Africa, he snubbed the trip to New Jersey on Sunday after a series of decisions taken by world football's governing body.Uefa reacted strongly after United States striker Folarin Balogun escaped a suspension following an intervention from US President Donald Trump.This was a key factor in Ceferin's decision not to go to the final. Uefa had criticised Fifa's decision at the time, calling it \"unprecedented, incomprehensible and unjustifiable\".\"When the certainty of rules is no longer guaranteed by its guardians, the integrity of the game is at stake and the credibility of a competition is undermined,\" Uefa said in a statement.Ceferin is also firmly against many other changes driven by Fifa president Gianni Infantino, including half-time shows. They will not be introduced to the Champions League or the European Championship.Pop giants Madonna, BTS, Shakira and Justin Bieber were among the acts to perform in the event, which extended the usual 15-minute half-time break to 27 minutes 22 seconds. Uefa is also strongly against a 64-team World Cup, which Infantino appeared to open the door to during the tournament.After referee Omar Artan was denied entry to the US before the tournament started, Uefa invited the Somali to officiate the Super Cup between Paris St-Germain and Aston Villa on 12 August.The high ticket prices have also proved controversial within Uefa. Uefa will broadly freeze ticket prices for Euro 2028 - meaning supporters will be able to buy five for the price of a single parking space at a World Cup stadium in the United States this summer.BBC Sport reported on Friday how Uefa has instructed its VARs not to consider diving under the new mistaken identity protocol, as Fifa has during the World Cup. This led to Switzerland striker Breel Embolo being sent off in the quarter-final defeat to Argentina.Players who cover their mouths in confrontations with opponents will not be shown red cards in Uefa competitions either.The issue of players covering their mouths became high profile in February when Benfica winger Gianluca Prestianni raised his shirt while speaking to Real Madrid's Vinicius Jr during a Champions League game.The Argentina international was accused of racist abuse and provisionally banned for one match. Following a Uefa investigation, Prestianni was found guilty of homophobic conduct and banned for six matches - three of which were suspended.The law change for a red card was the brainchild of Fifa president Infantino, who wanted something which would have \"a deterrent effect\" at the World Cup.Paraguay's Miguel Almiron was the first player to be sent off in his side's World Cup group game against Turkey. Arsenal defender Piero Hincapie then saw red in Ecuador's last-16 exit to co-hosts Mexico.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-20T16:44:02.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/89fb/live/5f5ae690-8457-11f1-8cf4-43aed99842b4.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cr59mg77ep2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-20-how-we-measured-ai-writing-across-arxiv-and-where-the-measur",
      "title": "How we measured AI writing across arXiv, and where the measurement breaks",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://unslop.run/blog/measuring-ai-writing-on-arxiv\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48981206\nPoints: 228\n# Comments: 156",
      "body": "We scored the full text of 12,750 arXiv papers and found that about a third of new ones read as machine-written. Here is the method, the results, and an honest account of the limitations.Share of new arXiv papers flagged as machine-written, 2021 to 2026, at a threshold calibrated so pre-ChatGPT papers flag at 0.4%. The eight slate points are the pre-LLM control months; the band is a bootstrap 95% interval.\nShare of papers flagged as machine-written by field, over the 12 months to July 2026 (about 300 papers per field).\nThere is a genre of headline that says \"N% of X is now AI,\" and most are not worth reading, because the detector behind the number also flags some share of genuine human writing. If a tool marks 40% of new papers as machine-written but also marks 20% of papers written before ChatGPT existed, the real story is the 20% nobody mentioned.\nSo we built the study around that objection. Our detector, described here, is calibrated for academic writing; at a 0.4% false-positive rate it clears 99.6% of genuine pre-LLM scientific text and recovers 85% of AI academic text. We made that false-positive rate the anchor. We took papers submitted in 2021 and 2022, before ChatGPT, treated them as ground-truth human, and set the flag threshold so that exactly 0.4% of them trip it. That line is the floor. Every number we report is a share of papers above a threshold where genuine pre-LLM writing sits, by construction, at 0.4%. The pre-ChatGPT years then act as a built-in control: if the rise were an artifact of the detector, 2021 and 2022 would flag as high as 2026. The first figure shows they do not.\nWe sampled ten field groups, roughly 25 papers per field per month, from January 2023 to July 2026, plus eight control months across 2021 and 2022, for 12,750 papers in total. For each one we pulled the version-1 PDF, so a paper revised in 2026 cannot leak modern text back into its 2023 slot. We scored the full body text instead of the abstract, because abstracts understate the signal: we have seen the same paper score under 20% on its abstract and over 70% on its body. Every reported figure carries a bootstrap 95% confidence interval.\nThe flagged share is flat at 0.4% through 2021 and 2022, lifts off within months of ChatGPT, and climbs in two waves to about 32% over the most recent complete quarter, peaking near 39% in early 2026. The spread across fields is large, and it is the table and the second figure that carry it. The values below are each field's flagged share over the 12 months to July 2026, alongside its pre-LLM control level.\nField groupPre-LLM controlRecent flagged share95% CIComputer science0.2%65.0%[59.3, 70.3]Quantitative biology3.5%56.3%[51.0, 61.7]Electrical eng. & systems1.7%51.3%[46.0, 57.0]Economics & finance2.5%47.0%[41.3, 52.7]Applied physics1.3%34.0%[29.0, 39.7]Statistics1.8%31.3%[26.0, 36.7]Condensed matter0.0%24.0%[19.3, 29.0]High-energy physics0.5%14.0%[10.0, 18.0]Astrophysics0.0%10.7%[7.3, 14.3]Mathematics0.0%0.7%[0.0, 1.7]\nComputer science leads at about 65%. Mathematics is lowest, near 0.7%, and the limitations section explains why its low value is hard to interpret. The control column is each field's 2021 to 2022 flag rate averaged over three sensitivity settings; the fields that rise most are not the ones with the highest pre-LLM control level, so an elevated starting point does not explain the rise.\nControl sample size. Each field's pre-ChatGPT control is 200 papers. At a 0.4% flag rate only eight papers flag across the entire 2,000-paper control, spread thinly over ten fields, so a single-threshold per-field control rate is coarse. The pooled floor is well estimated and is what the study is anchored to, but the per-field control levels are only approximate, and a larger control would not fix this: pinning a fraction-of-a-percent rate per field would require thousands of control papers per field that pre-2023 arXiv volume does not contain.\nA low score can indicate low adoption or a detector blind spot. Mathematics is the clearest case. Mathematics papers are dominated by notation and theorem-proof structure, and once equations and references are removed the remaining prose is sparse and unlike the scientific English the detector was trained on. A mathematics paper drafted with heavy model assistance may score low because its prose is out of distribution for the detector, so a low score in mathematics is weak evidence that a human wrote the paper. The result is consistent with two very different explanations, lower adoption or reduced detector sensitivity in that register, and this data cannot separate them. The fields with the strongest in-distribution assumption, the prose-heavy ones, are also the ones that rise most, so this confound does not account for the aggregate trend. But in the low-scoring fields the ranking should be read as a lower bound on adoption.\nDetector coverage. The detector is more sensitive to some generators than others, and we cannot evaluate it against the exact, private mixture of models and prompts that authors actually use. Incomplete coverage lowers the flag rate, so the reported prevalence is a lower bound: the true share is at least what we measured. The detector write-up reports the per-generator performance.\nA flag is not authorship. The detector estimates whether text reads as machine-written, at a calibrated probability with a known error rate. It cannot separate a lightly-edited document from a wholly-generated one, and a single score is never grounds to accuse a specific person. We report the prevalence of machine-like writing, which includes heavy AI-assisted editing.\nThe detector is cheap to run and we make no money from it. You can try it for free on any arXiv paper here, and on your own text here.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "dopamine_daddy",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-20T16:36:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://unslop.run/blog/measuring-ai-writing-on-arxiv",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-20-lg-8217-s-glossy-oled-gaming-monitor-is-rare-to-find-under-4",
      "title": "LG&#8217;s glossy OLED gaming monitor is rare to find under $400",
      "summary": "LG’s UltraGear Glossy OLED gaming monitor deliver deep, true-to-life blacks. | Image: LG\t\n\nIf you’ve been thinking about upgrading your gaming monitor, LG’s 27-inch 27GX704A-B pairs a glossy WOLED panel with a fast refresh rate, and it’s currently on sale for $379.99 (about $70 off) at Amazon and directly from LG, marking a new low price. It originally launched at $799.99, but has been available f",
      "body": "If you’ve been thinking about upgrading your gaming monitor, LG’s 27-inch 27GX704A-B pairs a glossy WOLED panel with a fast refresh rate, and it’s currently on sale for $379.99 (about $70 off) at Amazon and directly from LG, marking a new low price.&nbsp;It originally launched at $799.99, but has been available for under $500 since May.\n[Product: LG UltraGear Glossy OLED gaming monitor (27-inches) | https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FNQ4B2Z2?tag=theverge02-20, https://www.anrdoezrs.net/links/8836598/type/dlg/https://www.lg.com/us/monitors/lg-27gx704a-b-gaming-monitor]\nThe monitor offers a 1440p resolution and a glossy coating, which isn’t a combination of specs we often see at this price. Most OLED monitors under $400 use QD-OLED panels, which can give blacks a slight bluish tint. LG’s WOLED monitor delivers truer-looking blacks while the glossy coating helps colors appear richer and more vibrant.\nIt doesn’t sacrifice speed, either. The monitor boasts a fast 240Hz refresh rate with variable refresh rate support (Nvidia G-Sync compatible and AMD FreeSync Premium Pro), along with a fast 0.03ms response time to keep games looking sharp in motion.&nbsp;\nIt comes with a solid array of ports for the price, with two HDMI 2.1 ports, DisplayPort 1.4, and USB-A ports, making it easy to connect a PC, consoles, and accessories. Rounding things out, the stand is fully adjustable, too, allowing you to raise, lower, tilt, swivel, or rotate the display into portrait mode for a more comfortable setup. Or, because it has a VESA mount, you can yank it off and toss it onto a monitor arm of your choice.",
