      "id": "theverge-2026-07-20-lg-s-monitors-come-with-an-unwanted-addition-for-windows-mca",
      "title": "LG’s monitors come with an unwanted addition for Windows: McAfee pop-up ads",
      "summary": "This LG 34GX900A-B is one of the monitors found auto-installing LG’s app on users’ PCs. | Image: LG\t\n\nA video from Gamers Nexus explains how, after connecting a new LG UltraGear monitor to a PC running Windows 11 for the first time, Windows Update is silently installing LG driver updates and the LG Monitor App Installer, without so much as a permission pop-up or notification. The app doesn't appea",
      "body": "A video from Gamers Nexus explains how, after connecting a new LG UltraGear monitor to a PC running Windows 11 for the first time, Windows Update is silently installing LG driver updates and the LG Monitor App Installer, without so much as a permission pop-up or notification. The app doesn’t appear to include special controls of its own, instead simply pointing users to LG’s other apps, as well as McAfee antivirus.\nThere are reports about LG’s app auto-installing as far back as 2024, but Gamers Nexus’s test follows recent user complaints on Reddit about large pop-up ads for McAfee and LG’s own apps suddenly appearing in the bottom right corner of the homescreen, which popped up for GN on 31 out of 32 bootups.\n[Image: McAfee is shown alongside LG’s own apps on the Windows Store listing for LG Monitor App Installer. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/lg-monitor-app-installer-msoft-store-mcafee.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\n&nbsp;Microsoft allows developers to pair a Hardware Support App in the Windows Store that automatically installs based on the device’s metadata, but even its description here includes a warning:\n> The automatic installation feature does not provide a notification to the user when the app is installed. Some users may find this experience confusing and frustrating, and give your app a bad rating.\nAs The Verge’s Tom Warren has pointed out in the past, Dell similarly auto-installs its Alienware Command Center app on Windows when you connect an Alienware monitor, but the notification spam is an extra twist.\nUsers have reported ways to block LG’s app from auto-installing by applying a group policy setting on Pro versions of Windows, while users of Home editions might be able to turn off the installation by selecting “No” on an option to automatically download manufacturers’ apps and custom icons that is inside the Advanced system settings section.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "LG",
        "Microsoft",
        "News",
        "Privacy",
        "Tech",
        "Windows"
      ],
      "author": "Stevie Bonifield",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-20T16:14:58.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/lg-ultragear-34-inch-monitor.jpeg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,3.4613147178592,100,93.077370564282",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/967983/lg-monitors-mcafee-adware-gamers-nexus",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-20-ten-killed-as-russian-attacks-on-merchant-ships-in-black-sea",
      "title": "Ten killed as Russian attacks on merchant ships in Black Sea intensify",
      "summary": "Russian strikes on the Odesa region have killed 28 people in July alone, local authorities say.",
      "body": "The death toll of a Russian strike on a cargo ship off the coast of the Ukrainian city of Odesa has risen to 10. The Guinea-Bissau-flagged ship was hit by Russian cruise missiles on Sunday, the Ukrainian Air Force said, causing a fire.A search and rescue operation was launched and lasted all night. By Monday morning, Ukraine's seaports authority said nine crew members had been killed, as well as one of its pilots. India later said four of its citizens died in the attack.In recent weeks, the Odesa region has come under sustained attack. Twenty-eight people died in Russian strikes on the region in July alone, regional head Oleh Kiper told Ukrainian TV on Monday.Russian attacks on cargo ships in the Black Sea have also increased. Sunday's attack on the Guinea-Bissau-flagged Golden Leo ship was the deadliest of its kind. Among the victims were four Indian nationals, the country's foreign ministry said, adding that endangering crew members or impending freedom of navigation \"is deplorable and should be avoided\".Foreign-flagged ships like the Golden Leo exploit Ukraine's last remaining maritime export corridor to transport cargo down the Romanian and Bulgarian coastlines and onwards to Turkey. Maritime exports account for over two-thirds of Ukrainian agricultural exports, for a value of roughly $9bn (£6.7bn) - an essential source of revenue for Kyiv. Fighting in the Black Sea was at a stalemate for most of the four-and-a-half years since Moscow first launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine. While Ukrainian ships were unable to move further than the area surrounding Odesa due to Russian control of much of the coast, Kyiv's drones forced Moscow to keep its assets safely tucked in its naval bases in the eastern Black Sea.But Russia's ramping up of attacks against cargo ships is likely meant to deter foreign companies from buying Ukrainian products and using its Black Sea maritime corridor. On Sunday, Ukraine's Prime Minister Serhiy Koretskyi said his government and the military command were \"preparing decisions that will enhance the safety of navigation and help protect civilian vessels and seafarers\" from Russian attacks. Despite the constant shelling, Odesa remains a prime holiday destination for Ukrainians. Kiper said earlier this month that air raid shelters had been set up in 40 beaches in the region which were open to the public - but warned that seaside locations could be particularly dangerous because drones and missiles are often launched from the sea.For its part, Ukraine too has ramped up its attacks on Russian assets in the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov. On Monday, Ukraine's drone forces commander Robert Brovdi said 183 vessels had been struck in July. While this figure has not been independently verified, the BBC has seen evidence of damage to numerous vessels in both the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov on satellite.Elsewhere in the country, Russian strikes killed five people in separate attacks on eastern and southern Ukrainian cities.According to the UN, June saw the highest number of Ukrainian civilians killed and injured since April 2022.  At least 293 civilians were killed and 1,990 injured.For its part, Kyiv also continued to hit logistics facilities and an oil depot in Russia's Moscow region. Sergei Sobyanin, the mayor of the Russian capital, said Kyiv had launched 400 drones towards the region overnight on Monday, although most were shot down. Local authorities later said 10 people had been injured as a result.And local authorities in Russia's Belgorod border region said a Ukrainian drone strike on a bus had killed five people, including a child, on Monday. Ukraine has not yet commented.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-20T16:10:38.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/0aaf/live/6adb1450-843d-11f1-9cf5-33cd4d5fa4a6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c4gvpv3ewv2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-20-adobe-s-natural-look-camera-app-embraces-generative-ai",
      "title": "Adobe’s ‘natural look’ camera app embraces generative AI",
      "summary": "Welcome to the “what is a photo” debate, Adobe. | Image: Adobe\t\n\nAdobe's experimental camera app has taken an unexpected turn. After Project Indigo was launched last year to provide a \"more natural (SLR-like) look\" for iPhone photography, the Indigo camera app is now being updated with a suite of generative AI tools. And the change doesn't rely upon Adobe's own Firefly AI models.\nAdobe describes t",
      "body": "Adobe’s experimental camera app has taken an unexpected turn. After Project Indigo was launched last year to provide a “more natural (SLR-like) look” for iPhone photography, the Indigo camera app is now being updated with a suite of generative AI tools. And the change doesn’t rely upon Adobe’s own Firefly AI models.\nAdobe describes the new “AI Playground” tooling suite as an experiment and says there’s a button that allows users to opt out and continue using the app as before. Free access to the suite with no sign-on requirement is being tested with a small percentage of Indigo users over the next few weeks. “Our goal is to learn how people use GenAI-powered editing. Depending on its success we may extend the experiment, or expand the user pool, or run follow-on experiments,” Adobe fellow Marc Levoy says in the announcement blog. “If the feature proves popular, we will eventually offer a paid version.”\nThe Indigo app’s AI Playground, added with the version 1.1 update, includes four sections: Styles, Object Editing, Photo Guidance, and Custom Edit. Levoy says each section contains “multiple buttons or toggles, which trigger GenAI with prompts we have engineered for them.” If you don’t like the results of an edit, you can press the button again and “ typically get something slightly different.”\nStyles applies preset AI-generated filter effects to photographs snapped using Indigo, making them look like illustrations, changing the lighting effects, and more. Object Editing includes tools that remove people, objects, and other distractions from the backgrounds of images, with generative AI then used to fill in the blank spaces left behind, similar to Google’s Magic Eraser feature. Photo Guidance is a feedback feature that allows AI to analyze your photos to provide suggestions for reshoots and editing.\nThe last feature, Custom Edit, is a broader prompting tool that lets users describe how they want to manipulate an image in more detail. For example, if you’re unhappy with the results of a Styles preset, Custom Edit allows you to specify what else to change. Adobe is leaning on Google’s Nano Banana AI model for this update “to start,” but says it may switch or introduce additional model support over time.\n[Image: In this example, Adobe used the “Pen and ink” button in the Styles presets, followed by a Custom Edit to “Remove the fence in the foreground and the people in the backgroun.” https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/Screenshot-2026-07-20-110515.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nIt’s a far cry from the experience that Indigo provided at launch, with my colleague Allison describing it as a “camera app built by camera nerds for camera nerds.” It uses computational photography features to help mobile photographers capture better-quality images that re-create the look of SLR photos, alongside providing manual controls like focus, shutter speed, ISO, and white balance. The introduction of generative AI editing features may have been planned from the start, however.\n“This is the beginning of a journey for Adobe — towards an integrated mobile camera and editing experience that takes advantage of the latest advances in computational photography and AI,” Levoy said when the Indigo app first launched.\n[Image: Indigo’s Custom Edit tool can also be used to make paintings look like actual photographs, if that’s something you want to use a photography app for… https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/Screenshot-2026-07-20-112327.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nNotably, the experiment also lacks the same level of consideration toward AI transparency that Adobe usually exerts. Levoy says the Indigo team is working toward adding C2PA Content Credentials — a metadata standard for identifying AI content that’s already widely used by Adobe’s Firefly models — to images manipulated by Indigo’s generative AI tools and notes that Nano Banana already applies Google’s invisible SynthID watermark.\n“Whether the edits you apply are ‘fair’ or ‘misleading’ depend on what you do, who you share the images with, and how you present them.” Levoy says. “In the end, nothing can be implemented that reduces the importance of behaving responsibly.”",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
