      "id": "bbc-2026-07-19-how-the-world-cup-has-affected-your-premier-league-team",
      "title": "How the World Cup has affected your Premier League team",
      "summary": "Now Spain have been crowned world champions after victory over Argentina in New Jersey, attention will quickly shift to the new domestic season.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Bukayo Saka, Alexis Mac Allister and Kobbie Mainoo will return to Arsenal, Liverpool and Manchester United after very different World Cup experiencesNow Spain have been crowned world champions after victory over Argentina in New Jersey, attention will quickly shift to the new domestic season.The Premier League was well represented in North America, with all 20 of the top-flight teams for 2026-27 having at least one player involved.From injuries to transfers, here is how the World Cup has impacted your Premier League team.ArsenalImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, William Saliba went off during the first half of France's semi-final defeat by SpainArsenal may be the Premier League team affected most by the World Cup.The Gunners face an anxious wait over the fitness of William Saliba, who had been managing a back issue before his tournament was ended by the same problem.The centre-back has not yet been assessed by the club's medical team, but if he was sidelined, it would be a big loss - especially as his defensive partnership with Gabriel is so crucial to how the team play.The other two question marks will be over the fitness of Declan Rice and Bukayo Saka, who helped England finish third while managing their own fitness issues.It does not look like they will need a longer lay-off once the tournament is over, but they will be monitored closely by Arsenal before the new season.Leandro Trossard enjoyed a fine tournament with Belgium, starting all six games and scoring twice as they reached the quarter-finals, before the forward joined Turkish club Besiktas for £15m.Gabriel Martinelli, who showed flashes of his quality playing for Brazil, is another player the Gunners could be open to selling this summer.Carlo Ancelotti played the winger in a central role during the tournament, and that may be an extra attraction for potential buyers.Aston VillaImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Amadou Onana's knee injury has seriously impacted Aston Villa's preparationsAston Villa's transfer plans were thrown a curveball by an injury to Amadou Onana.The midfielder ruptured the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) in his right knee during Belgium's last-16 win over the USA which will rule him out until next year.They were not planning significant changes in midfield, although an extra body would have been useful, so moved for Johan Manzambi and Joao Gomes - especially with Youri Tielemans joining Manchester United.Manzambi - who has completed a club record move for more than £50m from Freiburg - excelled for Switzerland, scoring three goals, and will form part of Villa's new-look squad as Unai Emery tries to refresh it amid potential further sales.Chelsea have agreed a £117m deal to sign England midfielder Morgan Rogers, while France's Lucas Digne is due to join Paris St-Germain, but goalkeeper Emi Martinez, whose Argentina side lost to Spain in the final, is likely to stay.BournemouthImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Winger Rayan was a rare bright spot in a disappointing World Cup for BrazilByEmma SmithBBC Sport journalistBournemouth fans will have been encouraged by the performances of Ben Gannon-Doak at the World Cup, despite Scotland's brief stay in the tournament. The winger played in all three group matches, and particularly impressed in their sole win over Haiti.The Cherries had six players at the World Cup, including New Zealand back-up goalkeeper Alex Paulsen who has joined Motherwell on loan for the coming season.Starring among them, as well as Gannon-Doak, was winger Rayan. The 19-year-old started all Brazil's games bar their opener and claimed an assist in the 3-0 win over Scotland to seal their path to the knockout stage.It further raises Rayan's stock following his January move to Bournemouth, and superb performances in the second half of last season.This could be bad news for Bournemouth though, with Rayan heavily linked with Liverpool, managed by former Cherries boss Andoni Iraola.BrentfordImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Igor Thiago played only 62 minutes at the World Cup for Brazil, despite starting their opening fixtureByEmma SmithBBC Sport journalistFor a couple of Brentford players, the World Cup could not have gone much worse.Striker Igor Thiago came into the tournament on the back of a stellar scoring season for the Bees and tipped as Brazil's lead attacker in North America. However, after a lacklustre performance in the opener against Morocco he was dropped by manager Carlo Ancelotti and did not appear again.It remains to be seen what impact this has on Thiago's confidence heading into the new season.Meanwhile, midfielder Jordan Henderson made one substitute appearance for England, but his most memorable contribution was falling over an advertising hoarding during the celebrations following the win over Mexico and requiring surgery on a broken wrist.Henderson, however, stayed with the England squad and should be fit for the start of Brentford's season, though he may have to initially play in a cast.BrightonImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Brighton's new £46m defender Luka Vuskovic appeared in only one match for Croatia at the World CupByEmma SmithBBC Sport journalistBrighton went into the World Cup with eight representatives and emerged with eight - although the personnel has changed.Dutch centre-back Jan Paul van Hecke has moved to Tottenham from Brighton, with Croatia defender Luka Vuskovic going the other way.Vuskovic endured a difficult World Cup, subbed off after 66 minutes of Croatia's opening loss to England, then not appearing in their three subsequent games.Indeed, the only one of Brighton's eight representatives to make it past the last 16 was Belgium left-back Maxim de Cuyper who played in all six of their matches, starting five.This could be useful for Brighton, who will hope key players like Bart Verbruggen, Pascal Gross and Yasin Ayari will therefore not be too tired after the World Cup and fresh for the start of the Premier League season.ChelseaImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Chelsea captain Reece James struggled with a hamstring problem throughout the World CupChelsea had 11 players involved at the World Cup, but only seven played in the tournament.Key players Cole Palmer, Levi Colwill and Joao Pedro were left out of their respective squads and have already returned for pre-season training.The greatest concern has been the fitness of captain Reece James, who played into the latter stages of the tournament after helping England reach the third-place play-off, but suffered hamstring issues.Enzo Fernandez, who was sent off in the final against Spain, and Malo Gusto are set to return late for pre-season, along with expected new signing Valentin Barco.England midfielder Morgan Rogers is set to arrive at Chelsea from Aston Villa for that club record £117m deal, though international team-mate Trevoh Chalobah is expected to leave this summer.Much like Cristiano Ronaldo after Portugal knocked England out of the European Championship in 2006, Fernandez may find his antics in and around Argentina's victory over England will make him a target for opposition fans next season should he stay with Chelsea.CoventryImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Aurele Amenda has seven Switzerland caps - but did not add to them at the World CupNew Coventry defender Aurele Amenda suffered World Cup frustration. The centre-back, who has joined from Frankfurt, was an unused substitute in Switzerland's run to the quarter-finals.Striker Haji Wright played against Australia and Belgium as co-hosts USA bowed out to the Red Devils in the last 16.The 28-year-old will be one of Coventry's main threats as they return to the Premier League for the first time in 25 years, having scored 20 goals to help them to the Championship title last season.Fellow forward Brandon Thomas-Asante, who scored 13 goals for the Sky Blues last season, also featured in two of Ghana's World Cup games as they exited the competition in the last 32.Crystal PalaceImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Yeremy Pino played in two World Cup games for Spain before suffering a shoulder injuryByEmma SmithBBC Sport journalistCrystal Palace were one of the most represented Premier League clubs at the World Cup, with 10 players involved.They received a potential blow, however, when Spain winger Yeremy Pino sustained a shoulder injury during the group stage, and missed Spain's victory in the World Cup final.Elsewhere, goalscoring midfielder Daichi Kamada and attacking right-back Daniel Munoz impressed for Japan and Colombia respectively. New Palace manager Pierre Sage will hope the club rebuffs any potential suitors.Other key players like goalkeeper Dean Henderson and striker Jean-Philippe Mateta have been peripheral figures for England and France, so should be raring to go when they return for pre-season training.EvertonImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Iliman Ndiaye scored once and registered two assists for Senegal at the World CupIliman Ndiaye is one of Everton's biggest assets and was at the centre of transfer talk before the World Cup.He made an impact at the tournament with a spectacular strike after coming on as a substitute in Senegal's 5-0 win against Iraq. They reached the last 32 before losing 3-2 to Belgium.It remains to be seen whether the World Cup reignites speculation, but Everton will hope it stays quiet as Ndiaye is arguably their most creative player.Idrissa Gueye, Ndiaye's Senegal team-mate, had his Everton deal expire at the start of July, so is effectively a free agent.All talks on his future were shelved until the end of the World Cup, so a decision on the 36-year-old's next move will be made soon.Nathan Patterson played for Scotland at right-back, but he has failed to convince manager David Moyes that he should be a regular starter at Everton.The Toffees are actively pursuing targets in that position, so Patterson could yet be on the move before the start of the season.FulhamImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Luc de Fougerolles made five appearances for Canada at the World CupByCharlotte CoatesBBC Sport journalistIt was a World Cup to remember for four out of the six Fulham players involved.Sander Berge and Oscar Bobb were part of the brilliant Norway side beaten by England in the quarter-finals, while Belgium's Timothy Castagne and Morocco's Issa Diop also fell at that stage. While Bobb was not often a starter for Norway, his second-half appearances allowed him to showcase his talents and ability to move the ball in the final third. But for Fulham fans, it was a chance to catch a glimpse of defender Luc de Fougerolles.The 20-year-old has been at Craven Cottage since 2023 but is yet to make an appearance after spending last season on loan at Belgium Pro League side Dender EH.Perhaps new manager Alvaro Arbeloa will be pleased with what he saw. HullImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Liam Millar was a product of the Liverpool academy but failed to make any first-team appearancesByCharlotte CoatesBBC Sport journalistNewly-promoted Hull watched two players leave for the World Cup and welcomed home one. Croatia goalkeeper Ivor Pandur made the switch from the Tigers to Rangers for a fee of £6m at the end of June. But moving the other way from Ibrox was Jack Butland to fill the goalkeeping void. Hull fans did have something to cheer as Liam Millar progressed to the last 16 of the World Cup in a historic run for co-hosts Canada. Hull boss Sergej Jakirovic can look forward to having an almost completely fresh squad ready to tackle pre-season before the Premier League starts in August. IpswichImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, George Hirst was an unused substitute in all Scotland's World Cup matchesByCharlotte CoatesBBC Sport journalistLike fellow newly promoted Hull, Ipswich only had two players make their way to America, Canada and Mexico for the World Cup. Striker George Hirst was named in the first Scotland squad to reach the World Cup in 26 years, while Ali Al-Hamadi was there with Iraq.Hirst failed to make it off the bench for Scotland in a campaign that ended at the group stage, as Al-Hamadi appeared three times for winless Iraq. For new Ipswich manager Gary O'Neil, it presents a chance to put out his pre-season ideas to an almost full squad - including new signing Emersonn.The striker is the first Brazilian to sign for the Tractor Boys but, despite playing for his country at youth level, he is yet to win a senior cap. LeedsImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Ao Tanaka signed for Leeds in 2024 from Fortuna DusseldorfByCharlotte CoatesBBC Sport journalistLeeds fans could only keep an eye on four of their players at the World Cup - with new signing Harry Wilson's Wales failing to qualify.Left-back Gabriel Gudmundsson was the Leeds player who appeared the most times in the tournament, featuring in all four of Sweden's matches. Midfielder Ao Tanaka was left heartbroken by Japan's stoppage-time loss to Brazil in the last 32. He made three appearances for his nation - who many thought were dark horses for the tournament.Despite coming within a whisker of the semi-finals with Switzerland, Noah Okafor will return to Elland Road fresh, with just one World Cup appearance to his name. Leeds signed defender Tarik Muharemovic for £34.1m from Serie A side Sassuolo in the summer. The 23-year-old made three appearances for Bosnia-Herzegovina in the World Cup, including in the last-32 defeat by co-hosts the United States.With survival the aim for Daniel Farke's side last season, now it is all about  progression.  LiverpoolImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Alexis Mac Allister has been key in Argentina's eventful run to the World Cup finalLiverpool have a World Cup winner and runner-up in their squad after Spain beat Argentina in the final on Sunday.New signing Victor Munoz did not play a minute for Spain at the tournament but has a winner's medal. Alexis Mac Allister excelled for Argentina after a below-par season at Anfield, and his effective display against England will certainly have pleased Iraola.Aside from that, Liverpool will take plenty of confidence from the other players they had in North America. Alexander Isak scored once and got three assists for Sweden while Florian Wirtz provided three assists for Germany.Cody Gakpo scored three times for the Netherlands, with Virgil van Dijk and Ryan Gravenberch also playing for them.Meanwhile, Alisson played every minute for Brazil before they were knocked out by Norway in the last 16.In terms of signings, Liverpool have expressed interest in Yan Diomande, while Bradley Barcola is also on their shortlist. Both impressed for Ivory Coast and France respectively.Manchester CityImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, New Manchester City signing Elliot Anderson (right) was a key player for England in North AmericaManchester City midfielder Rodri played a starring role in captaining Spain to Sunday's World Cup final victory over Argentina. He was also named player of the tournament.The 29-year-old's long-term future at Etihad Stadium is uncertain as he enters the final year of his contract, and reports suggest he is set to miss the start of the season to have surgery on an unspecified injury.City hold a strong interest in Lille's Morocco midfielder Ayyoub Bouaddi after the teenager shone for the North Africans during the tournament, but it remains to be seen whether new manager Enzo Maresca makes a move for the 18-year-old who could cost up to £80m.Club record signing Elliot Anderson enhanced his reputation in England's run to the semi-finals and striker Erling Haaland seamlessly transferred his club form to the world stage, but Rayan Cherki found minutes hard to come by with France.Fifa has mandated three weeks off for players during the off-season, meaning the aforementioned City players will not be on the plane for the club's pre-season trip to the Far East unless joining later on.Manchester UnitedImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Kobbie Mainoo was one of only two England outfield players not to feature for a single minute in their first seven games of the 2026 World CupByChief football news reporterThe most concrete World Cup news for Manchester United was Manuel Ugarte's knee injury.Ugarte is going to be out until 2027 after having surgery to repair cruciate ligament damage. Given the Uruguay midfielder was likely to be sold this summer, it has added an extra layer of complication around United's recruitment plans.Then we have Kobbie Mainoo.The 21-year-old shrugged off the disappointment at being continually overlooked by Ruben Amorim to deliver excellent performances over the second half of last season, which suggests he can bounce back from not playing for England at the World Cup.However, Michael Carrick, who experienced the same thing under Fabio Capello in 2010, might need to put an arm around his shoulder.Argentina defender Lisandro Martinez went off injured in the World Cup final with what looked like a muscle injury, and United will be hoping the damage is not serious when he is fully assessed. NewcastleImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Dan Burn was deployed three times as a late substitute for England at the World CupAnthony Elanga and Yoane Wissa's performances for Sweden and DR Congo respectively will have offered Newcastle United a little encouragement.The pair even scored as many goals combined (five) at the World Cup as they did during their challenging debut campaign at St James' Park.Elsewhere, Newcastle captain Bruno Guimaraes may have missed a penalty in Brazil's last-16 defeat against Norway, but the midfielder still caught the eye by setting up four goals at the tournament.It is easy to see why Newcastle do not want to lose their talisman after he intimated he wished to join Arsenal.The sight of another midfielder, Johan Manzambi, impressing for Switzerland ultimately left Newcastle wondering what might have been after their top target opted to join Aston Villa instead.Newcastle have targeted young players with a similar profile this summer, but head coach Eddie Howe will rely on experienced leaders like Dan Burn to help them along.Burn, fresh from making his World Cup debut for England aged 34, still has a lot to offer.Nottingham ForestImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Sweden midfielder Lucas Bergvall is a £45m target for Nottingham ForestTottenham's Lucas Bergvall is a priority target for Nottingham Forest. They are looking to replace Elliot Anderson after his record move to Manchester City and Bergvall is their number one option.The midfielder helped Sweden to the last 32 of the World Cup, where they lost 3-0 to France.Winger Dan Ndoye was key in Switzerland's run to the quarter-finals and could flourish in Oliver Glasner's new team. He scored twice, including in Switzerland's quarter-final defeat by defending champions Argentina.Austria reached the last 32 with Xaver Schlager featuring and the midfielder is expected to join Forest on a free transfer after leaving RB Leipzig.Ibrahim Sangare helped Ivory Coast reach the last 32, where they lost to Norway, while Tyler Bindon and Chris Wood could not stop New Zealand exiting at the group stage.SunderlandImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Granit Xhaka (left) was the subject of transfer interest from ChelseaByCharlotte CoatesBBC Sport journalistSunderland were among the Premier League clubs with the highest number of players participating at the World Cup and it is no surprise given they secured European football last season. There were mixed results for the Black Cats contingent with captain Granit Xhaka narrowly missing out on a semi-final match against England. Chemsdine Talbi was a bright spark for Morocco when used and Brian Brobbey proved as difficult to play against as ever when leading the line for the Netherlands. Striker Wilson Isidor's journey ended at the group stage with Haiti but he will always have that stunning goal against Morocco to look back on. To boost their already exciting squad, Sunderland have added experienced Belgium right-back Thomas Meunier to their ranks. TottenhamImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Cristian Romero played in his second World Cup final for ArgentinaTottenham Hotspur's main transfer business was done before the World Cup, with arrivals such as Sandro Tonali, Mateus Fernandes, Andrew Robertson, plus central defenders Marcos Senesi and Jan Paul van Hecke.This has sparked talk of outgoings, which the tournament could have an impact on.It is the third World Cup in succession that Spurs were represented in the final, with Hugo Lloris playing for France in 2018 and 2022, while Argentina's Cristian Romero made his second appearance.The arrival of Senesi and Van Hecke has increased speculation about Spurs captain Romero's future, and his displays for Argentina will have further attracted the clubs who have been linked with him, including Atletico Madrid and Inter Milan.Romero has the ultimate showcase when Argentina face Spain in the World Cup final. Another strong performance might bring a move closer, as well as increasing Spurs' bargaining position.",
